      "id": "u1",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Redox reactions as coupled electron loss and gain",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Redox reactions (short for **reduction-oxidation**) are chemical reactions where electrons move from one substance to another. They’re everywhere—from why iron rusts to how your phone battery works and even how your body gets energy from food. The key idea is that one substance **loses** electrons while another **gains** them at the same time.\n\n### The Two Halves of Every Redox Reaction\n- **Oxidation** = loss of electrons (think “OIL” – Oxidation Is Loss).  \n  The atom or ion that loses electrons becomes more positive.\n- **Reduction** = gain of electrons (think “RIG” – Reduction Is Gain).  \n  The atom or ion that gains electrons becomes more negative.\n\nThese two processes are always paired—like two sides of the same coin. You can’t have one without the other.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "from why iron rusts to how your phone battery works and even how your body gets energy from food"
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Accurately defines redox reactions as coupled processes involving electron transfer, oxidation as loss of electrons leading to a more positive state, and reduction as gain of electrons leading to a more negative state.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u2",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Rusting of iron as an everyday redox reaction",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### Easy Real-Life Example: Rusting of Iron\nWhen iron reacts with oxygen in moist air, it forms rust (iron oxide).  \nSimplified equation:  \n**4 Fe + 3 O₂ → 2 Fe₂O₃**\n\n- Iron atoms lose electrons (they are oxidized).  \n- Oxygen atoms gain those electrons (they are reduced).",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "worked"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "When iron reacts with oxygen in moist air, it forms rust (iron oxide)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Correctly shows the simplified balanced reaction of iron rusting and identifies iron as oxidized and oxygen as reduced.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u3",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Zinc and copper sulfate displacement reaction worked example",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Another everyday example is a **displacement reaction** you might do in lab:  \n**Zn (zinc) + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu (copper)**\n\nHere, zinc metal gives away electrons to copper ions in solution. Zinc is oxidized; copper ions are reduced and turn into shiny copper metal that coats the zinc.",
          "occurrence": 0
        },
        {
          "text": "In the zinc-copper example:  \n- Zn goes from 0 → +2 (oxidized)  \n- Cu goes from +2 → 0 (reduced)  \n- Sulfur and oxygen stay the same.",
          "occurrence": 0
        },
        {
          "text": "In the zinc-copper reaction:  \n- Zinc is the **reducing agent** (it gives electrons away).  \n- Copper ions are the **oxidizing agent** (they accept electrons).",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "worked"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "Another everyday example is a **displacement reaction** you might do in lab"
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Accurately represents the single displacement reaction, tracking electron transfer, oxidation state changes from 0 to +2 for Zn and +2 to 0 for Cu, and identifying Zn as the reducing agent and Cu2+ as the oxidizing agent.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u4",
      "kind": "PROCEDURE",
      "label": "Method for identifying redox reactions using oxidation numbers",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### How to Spot a Redox Reaction\nChemists use **oxidation numbers** (or oxidation states) to track electron movement:\n\n1. An element that starts at 0 and ends with a positive number has been **oxidized** (lost electrons).  \n2. An element that starts at 0 and ends with a negative number has been **reduced** (gained electrons).  \n3. If oxidation numbers don’t change, it’s probably not a redox reaction.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {},
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Provides a correct introductory heuristic for spotting redox changes based on changes in oxidation state from zero to positive/negative values.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u5",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Definitions of oxidizing and reducing agents",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### Oxidizing and Reducing Agents\n- The substance that causes oxidation (by taking electrons) is the **oxidizing agent**.  \n- The substance that causes reduction (by giving electrons) is the **reducing agent**.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Correctly defines oxidizing agents as electron acceptors that cause oxidation, and reducing agents as electron donors that cause reduction.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
